window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-6210228644bb07cd"]={"key":"ny_main-6210228644bb07cd","group":"ny_main","uid":"6210228644bb07cd","rank":48,"title":"flintridge delivery date training pack","occupation":"non_retail_sales_supervisors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are a first-line supervisor of non-retail sales workers at Flintridge Engineering, and Mariam Haldorsen needs a revised training pack ready before next week's contract-renewal workshop for Eldergrove Partners. The current draft came back from legal review with two problems: reviewers disagree about whether “delivery date” means dispatch to the carrier or receipt by the customer, and the material blurs the sales procedure with legal interpretation when comparing England and Scotland. The working practice is also mid-change, so staff must understand what applies to legacy renewals during the transition and what applies to new or renewed agreements afterward. There is a difficult edge case in which sales needs to respond quickly to a customer while Sigrid Almeida's legal review requires the contractual basis for any date statement to be clear; the pack must make the resulting boundary usable without pretending to give legal advice. Using the attached `eldergrove_delivery_date_legal_review.pdf`, prepare `eldergrove_delivery_date_training_pack.docx` as the only deliverable and write it into the top level of the working directory you start in, using exactly that filename. Make the document a concise, presentation-style training pack for Eldergrove Partners sales staff and contracting stakeholders, with an editable facilitator workbook included in the same file. It must compare England and Scotland and the dispatch and customer-receipt interpretations, give one justified training recommendation, define the contested term for practical use, distinguish internal procedure from legal interpretation, cover the transition and end state, set an operational procedure and clear escalation boundary, and include a deadline plan for the workshop and changeover. Use the figures and dates in the attached review accurately, explain material changes between the periods, and keep every repeated figure aligned between the presentation material and workbook. Keep the whole document to no more than eight pages, make it usable by a facilitator without asking the author a question, and leave out legal authorities or unsupported claims that are not in the supplied review.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/eldergrove_delivery_date_legal_review.pdf` — Frozen internal legal-review brief containing the England and Scotland contract positions, definitions of dispatch and customer receipt, transition and end-state dates, comparable period figures, the edge-case boundary, deadlines, and named ownership roles needed to revise the training pack.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `eldergrove_delivery_date_training_pack.docx` — A concise presentation-style training pack with an editable facilitator workbook embedded in the same Word document. (Write the file at the top level of the solver's starting working directory.; Use exactly the specified filename.; Keep the complete document to no more than eight pages.; Include both learner-facing training material and an editable facilitator workbook.; Do not introduce unsupported legal authorities, legal conclusions, or facts outside the supplied review.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/eldergrove_delivery_date_legal_review.pdf","bytes":6997,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/dc263d9693044ef9aaf1fd6fdcd962eba4883172bab44f0a1adb55e490f042c4.pdf","sha256":"dc263d9693044ef9aaf1fd6fdcd962eba4883172bab44f0a1adb55e490f042c4","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":" FROZEN INTERNAL REVIEW\n\n Eldergrove Partners: delivery-date wording\n As of 14 June 2026 | Contract-renewal workshop brief\n\n          Scope                                                           Working issue\n          This review records the current internal template positions     Reviewers have used \"delivery date\" for two different\n          for this programme. It is not legal advice and does not state   contractual events: carrier handover and customer receipt.\n          universal English or Scottish legal rules.\n\n\n 1. Reviewed template positions\n England template: the current England renewal template expressly uses CUSTOMER RECEIPT as the delivery event.\n\n Scotland template: the current Scotland renewal template expressly uses DISPATCH TO THE CARRIER as the delivery\n event.\n\n Limitation: these are contractual template positions reviewed for this programme, not automatic rules determined solely by\n jurisdiction. Staff must use the signed wording for the actual agreement.\n\n 2. Local classifications and evidence\n DISPATCH - the date goods are handed to the carrier. Supporting evidence: a carrier collection or handover record.\n\n RECEIPT - the date the customer takes delivery. Supporting evidence: signed proof of delivery or a carrier delivery event.\n\n UNCONFIRMED - the signed wording is silent, conflicting, or ambiguous. Sales must not select a dispatch-versus-receipt\n contractual meaning.\n\n 3. Transition and end state\n Transition closes 30 June 2026. Legacy agreements remain administered according to their signed delivery definition during\n the transition.\n\n End state starts 1 July 2026. New or renewed agreements use the contract-defined delivery event, with the reviewed\n template wording made explicit. A silent or ambiguous clause remains UNCONFIRMED until legal review.\n\n\n\n\nFlintridge Engineering | Eldergrove Partners | Internal review                                                           14 June 2026 | Page 1\n\f 4. Comparable monthly classification audit\n The monthly samples are comparable internal classifications. They are not a longitudinal claim about the same contracts\n and do not prove a change in law.\n\n     Period                  Jurisdiction             RECEIPT      DISPATCH            UNCONFIRMED           Total\n\n     April 2026              England                  15           6                   3                     24\n\n     April 2026              Scotland                 9            5                   2                     16\n\n     April 2026              Combined                 24 (60.0%)   11 (27.5%)          5 (12.5%)             40\n\n     May 2026                England                  18           4                   2                     24\n\n     May 2026                Scotland                 11           3                   2                     16\n\n     May 2026                Combined                 29 (72.5%)   7 (17.5%)           4 (10.0%)             40\n\n\n 5. Review reading\n After review and template work, receipt increased by 5 records, dispatch decreased by 4, and unconfirmed decreased by 1\n from April to May. This is an internal classification shift, not evidence of a jurisdictional legal change.\n\n 6. Operating boundary for a prompt customer query\n When the signed clause is explicit, sales may communicate the contract-defined event using the corresponding evidence\n basis. A request to interpret unclear wording remains a legal escalation.\n\n For a silent, conflicting, or ambiguous clause, sales must not promise dispatch or receipt as the contractual date.\n Acknowledge the query without a date commitment, classify it UNCONFIRMED, and escalate to Sigrid Almeida by 16:00 the\n same day.\n\n Legal review is required for missing or ambiguous signed wording, conflicting contract evidence, or a request to make a\n legal interpretation.\n\n\n\n\nFlintridge Engineering | Eldergrove Partners | Internal review                                            14 June 2026 | Page 2\n\f 7. Workshop and changeover plan\n Pack ready: 19 June 2026 at 12:00. Renewal workshop: 22 June 2026. Transition close: 30 June 2026. End-state start: 1\n July 2026.\n\n      Owner                                    Responsibility\n\n      Mariam Haldorsen                         Sales enablement and workshop owner; owns the training pack and renewal workshop.\n\n      Sigrid Almeida                           Legal counsel for contractual ambiguity and legal-interpretation requests.\n\n      Darius Ekstrom                           England sales lead.\n\n      Imani Ekstrom                            Scotland sales lead.\n\n      Mahdi Mikkelsen                          CRM and process owner for the process update.\n\n\n Prepared for controlled operating guidance during the renewal workshop. This frozen brief should be distinguished from later legal or\n organisational developments.\n\n\n\n\nFlintridge Engineering | Eldergrove Partners | Internal review                                                              14 June 2026 | Page 3\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/eldergrove_delivery_date_training_pack.docx","bytes":47248,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/bd4900e4dacc7de865b31df0023c22d2942be06f417f86d432f8c4ae340279d9.docx","sha256":"bd4900e4dacc7de865b31df0023c22d2942be06f417f86d432f8c4ae340279d9","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"LEARNER MATERIAL | CONTRACT-RENEWAL WORKSHOP\nDelivery Date: Renewal Workshop Pack\nFor Eldergrove Partners sales staff and contracting stakeholders\n\nPurpose: prepare for the contract-renewal workshop and handle the contested delivery-date term in a controlled, practical way.\nBy the end of the workshop, participants can\n- distinguish DISPATCH, RECEIPT, and UNCONFIRMED as different contractual date bases.\n- check the signed agreement before recording or communicating a delivery-date basis.\n- respond promptly to a customer while reserving legal interpretation for contractual ambiguity.\nWORKING RECOMMENDATION\nUse the event expressly defined in the signed contract. Do not select dispatch or receipt merely from jurisdiction.\nWhat this pack is and is not\nThis is operational training guidance based on the frozen internal legal review as of 14 June 2026. It is not legal advice and does not state universal English or Scottish legal rules.\n1. Name it\nEvent and evidence\n2. Control it\nProcedure and boundary\n3. Change it\nTransition and audit\n4. Use it\nTiming and ownership\nLearner pages 1-5 are followed by an editable Facilitator Workbook on pages 6-8.\n\n\nLEARNER MATERIAL | 1 OF 4\n1 | Name the contractual event\nDispatch and customer receipt are different events. The evidence and timing follow the wording that is actually signed.\n\nDISPATCH\nDate goods are handed to the carrier.\nEvidence: carrier collection or handover record.\nRECEIPT\nDate the customer takes delivery.\nEvidence: signed proof of delivery or carrier delivery event.\nUNCONFIRMED\nSigned wording is silent, conflicting, or ambiguous. Sales may not choose a dispatch-versus-receipt contractual meaning.\nWhy the distinction matters\n- DISPATCH happens at carrier handover; RECEIPT happens when the customer takes delivery. They are not interchangeable synonyms.\n- The chosen contractual event determines what sales records and what evidence supports a customer-facing statement.\nReviewed template comparison\nJurisdiction\nCurrent reviewed renewal template\nPractical takeaway\nEngland\nExpressly uses customer receipt as the delivery event.\nUse RECEIPT only where the signed agreement defines it.\nScotland\nExpressly uses dispatch to the carrier as the delivery event.\nUse DISPATCH only where the signed agreement defines it.\nCONTROL\nThe jurisdiction identifies the reviewed template context; it does not select the event for an agreement. Signed wording controls.\n\n\nLEARNER MATERIAL | 2 OF 4\n2 | Follow this controlled response\nUse the signed clause first. Keep operational action separate from legal interpretation.\n\nStep\nSales action\nRecord / communicate\n1\nCheck the signed contract wording.\nDo not infer the delivery event from a label or prior practice.\n2\nIdentify the applicable jurisdiction or reviewed template context.\nUse this as a control check, not as a jurisdictional default.\n3\nIf explicit: map the clause to its evidence.\nDISPATCH -> carrier handover record. RECEIPT -> proof of delivery or carrier delivery event.\n4\nIf silent, conflicting, or ambiguous: classify UNCONFIRMED.\nDo not select dispatch or receipt; route for legal review.\nUseable boundary\nSALES DOES\nChecks signed wording and jurisdiction/template; records the correct local classification and evidence; communicates an explicit contract-defined event.\nLEGAL CONFIRMS\nMissing or ambiguous wording, conflicting contract evidence, and requests to make a legal interpretation of unclear wording.\nWhen a customer needs an answer quickly\nACKNOWLEDGE\n\"Thank you for your query. We are checking the contract-defined delivery-date basis and will update you.\" Do not make a date commitment for an UNCONFIRMED clause.\nEscalate to Sigrid Almeida by 16:00 the same day for missing or ambiguous signed wording, conflicting contract evidence, or a request for legal interpretation.\n\n\nLEARNER MATERIAL | 3 OF 4\n3 | Manage the change with the contract\nThe transition preserves signed legacy definitions; the end state makes the reviewed contract-defined approach explicit.\n\nTHROUGH 30 JUNE 2026\nTransition period\nLegacy agreements remain governed by their signed delivery definition.\nFROM 1 JULY 2026\nEnd state\nNew or renewed agreements use the contract-defined delivery event, with reviewed template wording explicit. A silent or ambiguous clause remains UNCONFIRMED until legal review.\nComparable monthly classification audit\nPeriod\nJurisdiction\nReceipt\nDispatch\nUnconfirmed\nTotal / combined % R-D-U\nApril\nEngland\n15\n6\n3\n24\nApril\nScotland\n9\n5\n2\n16\nApril\nCombined\n24\n11\n5\n40 | 60.0%/27.5%/12.5%\nMay\nEngland\n18\n4\n2\n24\nMay\nScotland\n11\n3\n2\n16\nMay\nCombined\n29\n7\n4\n40 | 72.5%/17.5%/10.0%\nApril combined: RECEIPT 60.0%, DISPATCH 27.5%, UNCONFIRMED 12.5%. May combined: RECEIPT 72.5%, DISPATCH 17.5%, UNCONFIRMED 10.0%.\nREAD IT CORRECTLY\nAcross the comparable 40-record monthly samples, receipt increased by 5, dispatch decreased by 4, and unconfirmed decreased by 1 after internal review and template work. This does not prove a change in law or track the same contracts.\n\n\nLEARNER MATERIAL | 4 OF 4\n4 | Put timing and ownership into action\nThe workshop is a handoff point: prepare the pack, close the transition, then use the end-state process.\n\nDeadline plan\nWhen\nMilestone\nOwner and handoff\n19 June 2026 at 12:00\nPack ready\nMariam Haldorsen owns training-pack readiness and workshop preparation.\n22 June 2026\nRenewal workshop\nMariam owns the workshop; Darius Ekstrom leads England sales input and Imani Ekstrom leads Scotland sales input.\n30 June 2026\nTransition close\nMahdi Mikkelsen owns the CRM and process update; sales leads reinforce the signed-definition control.\n1 July 2026\nEnd-state start\nNew or renewed agreements use the reviewed contract-defined approach; Sigrid remains the legal route for ambiguity.\nNamed responsibilities\nMariam Haldorsen\nSales enablement and workshop owner\nSigrid Almeida\nLegal counsel for contractual ambiguity\nDarius Ekstrom\nEngland sales lead\nImani Ekstrom\nScotland sales lead\nMahdi Mikkelsen\nCRM and process owner\nAll sales staff\nFollow the signed clause, capture evidence, and route interpretation requests.\nBefore any customer-facing date statement\n- Signed wording checked; applicable jurisdiction or template context identified; event and evidence matched.\n- If any wording or evidence conflicts, record UNCONFIRMED, acknowledge without a date commitment, and route to Sigrid by 16:00 the same day.\n\n\nEDITABLE FACILITATOR WORKBOOK | 1 OF 3\nFacilitator guide\nUse this workbook with the learner material. All text and capture areas are editable for a later workshop.\n\nSet-up and framing\n- State that the source is the frozen internal review as of 14 June 2026; do not introduce legal authorities or describe the reviewed template positions as settled law.\n- Keep the working recommendation visible: use the event expressly defined in the signed contract, not a jurisdictional assumption.\n- Ask participants to use the terms DISPATCH, RECEIPT, and UNCONFIRMED consistently in their answers.\nPrompt and answer cue\nAsk\nA sound response includes\nWhat differs between the reviewed England and Scotland templates?\nEngland's current renewal template expressly uses customer receipt; Scotland's expressly uses dispatch to the carrier. Both remain contract-specific template positions, and the signed wording controls.\nHow do dispatch and receipt differ in practice?\nDISPATCH is handover to the carrier, supported by a carrier collection or handover record. RECEIPT is when the customer takes delivery, supported by signed proof of delivery or a carrier delivery event.\nWhat applies through 30 June and from 1 July?\nLegacy agreements remain governed by their signed delivery definition through 30 June 2026. From 1 July 2026, new or renewed agreements use the reviewed contract-defined approach; silent or ambiguous wording remains UNCONFIRMED pending legal review.\nWhat should sales do when a customer needs a quick answer but wording is unclear?\nAcknowledge promptly without a date commitment; classify UNCONFIRMED; escalate to Sigrid Almeida by 16:00 the same day. Sales does not interpret unclear wording.\nFacilitator notes / questions to revisit\nNote or question\nFollow-up / owner\n\n\n\n\nEDITABLE FACILITATOR WORKBOOK | 2 OF 3\nApplied activity: choose the event or the boundary\nAsk learners to identify the signed contractual event, evidence, and customer-response boundary. Capture answers in the editable cells.\n\nCase\nPrompt\nParticipant response / facilitator capture\nA\nEngland\nA current England renewal agreement's signed clause expressly uses customer receipt. What classification applies, what evidence supports it, and why is jurisdiction not enough on its own?\n\nB\nScotland\nA current Scotland renewal agreement's signed clause expressly uses dispatch to the carrier. What classification applies, what evidence supports it, and what may sales communicate?\n\nC\nAmbiguous\nA legacy agreement in either location has silent or conflicting signed wording. A customer asks urgently which delivery date applies. What is the classification, acknowledgement, escalation destination, and deadline?\n\nAnswer cues after discussion\nA | RECEIPT\nCustomer takes delivery; support with signed proof of delivery or carrier delivery event. The signed clause chooses the event.\nB | DISPATCH\nGoods handed to carrier; support with carrier collection or handover record. Sales may communicate the explicit contract-defined event.\nC | UNCONFIRMED\nDo not choose dispatch or receipt. Acknowledge without a date commitment and escalate to Sigrid Almeida by 16:00 the same day.\nDebrief prompt: What changes at the end state? Expected answer: from 1 July 2026, new or renewed agreements use the reviewed contract-defined approach; ambiguity still remains UNCONFIRMED pending legal review.\n\n\nEDITABLE FACILITATOR WORKBOOK | 3 OF 3\nClose-out and reusable capture\nUse the final discussion to confirm the control, audit reading, and next questions without turning training guidance into legal advice.\n\nShow-me checklist\nListen for\nObserved / notes\nSigned wording checked before a date basis is recorded or communicated.\n\nJurisdiction or template context identified, without treating it as a legal default.\n\nExplicit dispatch clause linked to carrier handover; explicit receipt clause linked to proof of delivery or carrier delivery event.\n\nSilent, conflicting, or ambiguous wording classified UNCONFIRMED and routed to legal.\n\nTransition through 30 June 2026 and end state from 1 July 2026 distinguished.\n\nAudit reading cue\nCOMPARE\nApril combined: 24 RECEIPT, 11 DISPATCH, 5 UNCONFIRMED (40 total). May combined: 29 RECEIPT, 7 DISPATCH, 4 UNCONFIRMED (40 total). State the internal classification shift, not a change in law.\nQuestions and follow-up capture\nQuestion / issue\nRoute or owner\nFollow-up capture\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFacilitator source note: eldergrove_delivery_date_legal_review.pdf, frozen internal review as of 14 June 2026. Escalation questions on contractual ambiguity belong with Sigrid Almeida.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is a single editable Word document named `eldergrove_delivery_date_training_pack.docx`.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Submitted filename and document properties.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact top-level filename and that the file is a valid .docx rather than a renamed PDF or image."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document opens and renders without corruption, missing content, or unusable blank pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX.","grader_hint":"Open or convert the document offline and inspect the rendered pages for intact text, tables, and headings."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete document is no more than eight pages.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render with a standard office engine and count pages; do not treat a hidden overflow page as compliant."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The workbook portion contains genuinely editable Word text and table or form content rather than screenshots of facilitator material.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"DOCX XML and rendered workbook section.","grader_hint":"Select, search, or inspect document XML for editable paragraphs and table cells in the facilitator section."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The artifact functions as both a learner-facing presentation-style training pack and an embedded facilitator workbook, with the two uses distinguishable to a reader.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document structure and section labels.","grader_hint":"Check that a participant can follow the instructional material and a facilitator can find preparation, prompts, or capture areas without guessing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The intended audience is explicitly identified as Eldergrove Partners sales staff and/or contracting stakeholders, not merely a generic audience.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title, audience statement, or opening training page.","grader_hint":"Look for the named organisation and the relevant sales or contracting audience."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The material makes clear that its purpose is preparation for the contract-renewal workshop and practical handling of the contested delivery-date term.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening purpose and learning framing.","grader_hint":"A reviewer should be able to tell what the workshop is for and why the pack exists without reading the source PDF."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The England and Scotland comparison agrees with the supplied review: the jurisdiction-specific template positions are reported accurately, and the document does not convert those positions into universal rules of law.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdiction comparison against `eldergrove_delivery_date_legal_review.pdf`.","grader_hint":"Verify each jurisdiction/template statement against the PDF and separately check that the text preserves the review's limitation that the positions are contract-specific."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document defines dispatch as the date goods are handed to the carrier and identifies a carrier collection or handover record as its supporting evidence.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions or evidence section.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording, but both the carrier handover event and evidence concept must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document defines customer receipt as the date the customer takes delivery and identifies a signed proof of delivery or carrier delivery event as its supporting evidence.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions or evidence section.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording, but the customer-side delivery event and supporting evidence must both be stated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The comparison treats dispatch and customer receipt as distinct possible contractual events and explains why the distinction matters operationally, rather than presenting them as interchangeable synonyms.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison and operational implications.","grader_hint":"Look for a clear contrast in event timing, evidence, or date capture; a mere glossary list is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The training pack makes exactly one clear working recommendation: teach staff to use the event expressly defined in the signed contract, without selecting dispatch or receipt merely from jurisdiction.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation statement.","grader_hint":"Award when there is one unambiguous call consistent with the supplied review; do not award if the document leaves multiple competing recommendations or chooses a jurisdictional default unsupported by the review."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation and training language clearly state that the pack is operational guidance rather than legal advice and do not claim that either interpretation is universally required by English or Scottish law.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope or caution language.","grader_hint":"Check both positive framing as internal guidance and absence of a universal legal conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Sales actions are visibly separated from legal interpretation, so staff can see what they are expected to record or communicate and what requires legal confirmation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Procedure, boundary, or labeled content.","grader_hint":"The distinction must be usable in the document, not just implied by a disclaimer."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The transition period is stated as ending on 30 June 2026, and the material says that legacy contracts remain governed by their signed delivery definition during that period.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition guidance compared with the supplied review.","grader_hint":"Verify the date and legacy-treatment relationship; do not accept a generic reference to 'the transition'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The end state is stated as beginning on 1 July 2026, with new or renewed agreements using the reviewed contract-defined approach rather than an inferred jurisdictional default.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state guidance compared with the supplied review.","grader_hint":"Check both the effective date and the described change in practice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The period comparison explains the material operational movement in the supplied audit: receipt classifications increase, dispatch classifications decrease, and unconfirmed classifications decrease from April to May, without presenting the sample movement as proof that the law changed.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period comparison and explanatory narrative.","grader_hint":"Require an explanation tied to the internal review/template-change context and reject a claim that the counts demonstrate a change in jurisdictional law."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The April audit figures reconcile to the supplied review: England has 15 receipt, 6 dispatch, and 3 unconfirmed records; Scotland has 9 receipt, 5 dispatch, and 2 unconfirmed records; the combined total is 40.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"April figures checked against the source PDF.","grader_hint":"Check each jurisdiction/category cell or stated equivalent and verify that the jurisdiction subtotals and combined total reconcile."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The May audit figures reconcile to the supplied review: England has 18 receipt, 4 dispatch, and 2 unconfirmed records; Scotland has 11 receipt, 3 dispatch, and 2 unconfirmed records; the combined total is 40.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"May figures checked against the source PDF.","grader_hint":"Check each jurisdiction/category cell or stated equivalent and verify that the jurisdiction subtotals and combined total reconcile."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any stated combined-period percentages are calculated correctly from the supplied counts: April receipt 60.0%, dispatch 27.5%, unconfirmed 12.5%, and May receipt 72.5%, dispatch 17.5%, unconfirmed 10.0%, each within ±0.1 percentage points.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Displayed percentages and source counts.","grader_hint":"Recalculate each percentage from the relevant 40-record total; do not penalize the artifact for omitting percentages, but score any percentages it presents for accuracy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"All figures, percentages, labels, and dates repeated between the learner-facing material and the facilitator workbook agree with one another and with the supplied review.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Repeated content within the single DOCX.","grader_hint":"Compare every repeated number/date and the meanings of `DISPATCH`, `RECEIPT`, and `UNCONFIRMED`; a mismatch in either embedded section loses this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The operational procedure requires checking the signed contract wording and identifying the applicable jurisdiction or template before a delivery-date basis is communicated or recorded.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Procedure content.","grader_hint":"Look for both contract-wording review and jurisdiction/template identification as explicit controls, not an implied general instruction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure links an explicit dispatch clause to a carrier handover record and an explicit receipt clause to proof of delivery or the carrier delivery event.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Procedure and evidence-handling content.","grader_hint":"Both branches and their evidence must be connected; a list of evidence types without the branch relationship is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The procedure gives a distinct treatment for silent, conflicting, or ambiguous wording: classify it as unconfirmed, avoid choosing dispatch or receipt as the contractual meaning, and route it for review.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ambiguity branch and local label from the source.","grader_hint":"Verify the three linked controls and accept `UNCONFIRMED` or an unmistakably equivalent label."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The edge-case guidance resolves the collision between a prompt customer response and the need for a confirmed contractual basis in a way that preserves a timely acknowledgement without making an unsupported delivery-date commitment.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Edge-case example, facilitator answer cue, or escalation guidance.","grader_hint":"The response must address both competing requirements; a blanket 'wait for legal' or a made-up date does not satisfy the trade-off."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The escalation boundary identifies the triggers that require legal review, including missing or ambiguous signed wording, conflicting contract evidence, and a request to make a legal interpretation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Escalation section or decision aid.","grader_hint":"Require clear triggers rather than a vague instruction to escalate 'when necessary'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The escalation guidance names Sigrid Almeida or the legal-counsel role as the destination and preserves the supplied same-day 16:00 escalation boundary for the ambiguous-date case.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Escalation owner and timing compared with the source PDF.","grader_hint":"Check both destination and time boundary; equivalent role wording is acceptable when the named owner is absent only if the role is unambiguous."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deadline plan includes the 19 June 2026 at 12:00 pack-ready deadline, the 22 June 2026 workshop, the 30 June 2026 transition close, and the 1 July 2026 end-state start in a coherent sequence.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Deadline plan checked against the source PDF.","grader_hint":"Verify all four milestones and their chronological relationship."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deadline plan assigns the supplied responsibilities coherently: Mariam Haldorsen for the training-pack/workshop ownership, Sigrid Almeida for legal ambiguity, Darius Ekstrom for England sales, Imani Ekstrom for Scotland sales, and Mahdi Mikkelsen for the process or CRM update.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Owners and handoffs compared with the source PDF.","grader_hint":"Accept a clear responsibility table or equivalent prose, but do not accept names listed without associated responsibilities."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The learner-facing material is instructional rather than a memo: it states what participants should be able to distinguish or do and uses concise explanations suitable for a renewal workshop.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Training pages and learning framing.","grader_hint":"Look for teachable objectives and explanations, not merely a data dump or legal-review summary."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The facilitator workbook provides usable prompts and answer cues for teaching the jurisdiction comparison, the two date interpretations, the transition/end state, and the ambiguous edge case.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Facilitator notes and exercise guidance.","grader_hint":"A facilitator should know what to ask and what a sound response must contain; generic 'discuss this' prompts do not qualify."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The workbook includes at least one practical activity or scenario that asks learners to distinguish an explicit contract event from an unconfirmed or ambiguous case and to apply the England/Scotland comparison.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editable exercise or scenario in the workbook.","grader_hint":"The activity must exercise the actual contested distinction and jurisdictional context, not just ask learners to recall definitions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The workbook includes editable places for facilitator notes, participant responses, or questions and follow-up capture that could be reused in another workshop.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Editable tables, prompts, or form fields.","grader_hint":"Inspect the rendered and underlying document for practical writable areas; static instructional prose alone is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The artifact identifies the supplied review as its source or states the relevant as-of date of 14 June 2026, allowing a facilitator to distinguish the frozen review from later legal developments.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Source note, footer, or facilitator reference.","grader_hint":"Accept either an explicit source filename/reference or the exact as-of date, provided the status of the information is clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document avoids unsupported legal authorities, invented thresholds, fabricated facts, and claims that the supplied internal positions are settled law.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole artifact reviewed against the supplied PDF.","grader_hint":"Judge whether every material factual or legal-sounding claim is supported by the source or is clearly framed as internal operating guidance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A facilitator and a sales participant can navigate the artifact and use it without asking the author to explain the recommendation, transition boundary, evidence expectations, escalation trigger, or workshop timing.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-to-end usability review of the rendered document.","grader_hint":"Assess clarity, sequencing, labels, legibility, and practical completeness; technically correct but confusing or contradictory material should not receive credit."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is an enterprise contract-renewal training revision for sales staff and contracting stakeholders working across England and Scotland. The document must teach a controlled interim practice while accurately showing what changes at the end state, without turning internal sales guidance into legal advice.","difficulty_sources":["Reconcile two contractual interpretations across England and Scotland without turning template positions into universal legal rules.","Explain a mid-change practice for both legacy agreements and the post-1 July end state.","Resolve the edge case between prompt customer acknowledgement and withholding an unsupported delivery-date commitment.","Transform one consistent review source into concise instructional material and a reusable facilitator workbook while keeping figures aligned."],"authoring_plan":{"source_file":{"filename":"eldergrove_delivery_date_legal_review.pdf","type":"frozen internal legal-review brief","as_of_date":"14 June 2026","content_constraints":["This is the sole supplied factual source.","The review describes internal contract-template positions for Eldergrove Partners and must not purport to state universal English or Scottish law.","Use the local classifications DISPATCH, RECEIPT, and UNCONFIRMED in the source so the solver can reproduce them without guessing a private code list."],"people_and_roles":{"Mariam Haldorsen":"sales enablement and workshop owner","Sigrid Almeida":"legal counsel for contractual ambiguity","Darius Ekstrom":"England sales lead","Imani Ekstrom":"Scotland sales lead","Mahdi Mikkelsen":"CRM and process owner"},"definitions":{"DISPATCH":"The date goods are handed to the carrier, supported by a carrier collection or handover record.","RECEIPT":"The date the customer takes delivery, supported by signed proof of delivery or a carrier delivery event.","UNCONFIRMED":"The date basis where signed wording is silent, conflicting, or ambiguous and no dispatch-versus-receipt meaning may be selected by sales."},"jurisdiction_positions":{"England":"The current England renewal template expressly uses customer receipt as the delivery event.","Scotland":"The current Scotland renewal template expressly uses dispatch to the carrier as the delivery event.","limitation":"These are contractual template positions reviewed for this programme, not automatic rules determined solely by jurisdiction. Staff must use the signed wording for the actual agreement."},"transition_and_end_state":{"transition_close":"30 June 2026","end_state_start":"1 July 2026","transition_rule":"Legacy agreements remain administered according to their signed delivery definition during the transition.","end_state_rule":"New or renewed agreements use the contract-defined delivery event, with the reviewed template wording made explicit; a silent or ambiguous clause remains UNCONFIRMED until legal review."},"period_audit":{"April 2026":{"England":{"RECEIPT":15,"DISPATCH":6,"UNCONFIRMED":3,"total":24},"Scotland":{"RECEIPT":9,"DISPATCH":5,"UNCONFIRMED":2,"total":16},"combined":{"RECEIPT":24,"DISPATCH":11,"UNCONFIRMED":5,"total":40}},"May 2026":{"England":{"RECEIPT":18,"DISPATCH":4,"UNCONFIRMED":2,"total":24},"Scotland":{"RECEIPT":11,"DISPATCH":3,"UNCONFIRMED":2,"total":16},"combined":{"RECEIPT":29,"DISPATCH":7,"UNCONFIRMED":4,"total":40}},"interpretation":"The comparable monthly samples show an internal classification shift after review and template work: receipt increased by 5 records, dispatch decreased by 4, and unconfirmed decreased by 1. The samples are not a longitudinal claim about the same contracts and do not prove a change in law.","derived_combined_percentages":{"April":{"RECEIPT":"60.0%","DISPATCH":"27.5%","UNCONFIRMED":"12.5%"},"May":{"RECEIPT":"72.5%","DISPATCH":"17.5%","UNCONFIRMED":"10.0%"}}},"edge_case_and_boundary":{"collision":"Sales is expected to acknowledge a customer query promptly, while legal requires the contractual basis for any delivery-date statement to be clear.","required_operating_boundary":"For a silent, conflicting, or ambiguous clause, sales must not promise dispatch or receipt as the contractual date. The query is acknowledged without a date commitment, classified UNCONFIRMED, and escalated to Sigrid Almeida by 16:00 the same day.","explicit_clause_boundary":"When the signed clause is explicit, sales may communicate the contract-defined event using the corresponding evidence basis; a request to interpret unclear wording remains a legal escalation."},"milestones":{"pack_ready":"19 June 2026 at 12:00","renewal_workshop":"22 June 2026","transition_close":"30 June 2026","end_state_start":"1 July 2026"}},"gold_requirements":["The gold DOCX contains a concise learner-facing training component and an editable facilitator workbook in one file and stays within the eight-page cap.","It makes one recommendation: teach the signed-contract-defined event, not a jurisdictional assumption; use the explicit England or Scotland template event where applicable and keep ambiguous cases UNCONFIRMED.","It defines DISPATCH, RECEIPT, and UNCONFIRMED using the source evidence descriptions.","It compares England and Scotland accurately while stating that the template positions are not universal law.","It explains the transition through 30 June 2026 and the end state from 1 July 2026.","It reproduces or accurately summarizes the April and May counts and any percentages, with reconciled totals and no drift between learner and facilitator sections.","It gives a usable operational path covering signed wording, jurisdiction/template, evidence, ambiguity, communication boundary, legal escalation, and the 16:00 same-day escalation deadline.","It includes the four milestone dates and the five named responsibility assignments from the source.","The workbook includes learning objectives or agenda framing, facilitator prompts with answer cues, the edge-case activity, and editable note or response capture areas.","It does not add external authorities, unsupported legal claims, invented thresholds, or facts not in the source."],"gold_derivation":"Build the frozen source with the exact definitions, roles, dates, audit counts, totals, and edge-case rule above. Derive the gold's numerical checks directly from the supplied counts: April totals are 24, 11, and 5 out of 40; May totals are 29, 7, and 4 out of 40; the percentage values are the corresponding totals divided by 40. Review the rendered gold against every rubric predicate, including page count, editability, internal consistency, and the professional usability judgment.","offline_validation":["Generate the PDF source and DOCX gold using offline standard office tooling.","Render the DOCX offline to verify that it opens, remains within eight pages, and contains readable learner and facilitator sections.","Search the DOCX text and tables for all required dates, labels, names, figures, and boundary language.","Compare repeated figures and dates within the single DOCX and compare all source-dependent claims with the frozen PDF.","Have a domain-aware reviewer assess whether the material teaches rather than merely summarizes and whether it avoids presenting internal template positions as law."]},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"ref_delivery_date_legal_review","filename":"eldergrove_delivery_date_legal_review.pdf","purpose":"Frozen internal legal-review brief containing the England and Scotland contract positions, definitions of dispatch and customer receipt, transition and end-state dates, comparable period figures, the edge-case boundary, deadlines, and named ownership roles needed to revise the training pack.","required":true,"discovery":"Use the attached PDF as the sole factual source for the jurisdiction comparison, dates, figures, local labels, and named responsibilities."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"revised_training_pack","filename":"eldergrove_delivery_date_training_pack.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A concise presentation-style training pack with an editable facilitator workbook embedded in the same Word document.","required":true,"constraints":["Write the file at the top level of the solver's starting working directory.","Use exactly the specified filename.","Keep the complete document to no more than eight pages.","Include both learner-facing training material and an editable facilitator workbook.","Do not introduce unsupported legal authorities, legal conclusions, or facts outside the supplied review."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"training_material","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"non_retail_sales_supervisors","sector":"wholesale_trade","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Use one domain-aware reviewer and one independent document-quality reviewer. Both review the rendered DOCX against the supplied PDF and the rubric; the domain-aware reviewer focuses on contractual framing, operational boundary, and instructional accuracy, while the independent reviewer focuses on reconciliation, usability, editability, and page limit.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The jurisdiction positions are intentionally fictional internal template positions supplied for this workshop and are not a substitute for current legal advice.","The source is a frozen offline review, so validation concerns only the supplied facts and not later legal or organisational changes."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:13:50+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:13:50+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:13:50+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T15:13:50+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/6210228644bb07cd","declared_deliverables":["eldergrove_delivery_date_training_pack.docx"]};
